{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And he came also to Rachel.</b> The reason for his saying <b>gam</b> twice is — or perhaps it intends to include that he came with added permanence with her, and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 70:3) that his bed was permanently with Rachel, and therefore it says וַיָּבֹא גַּם אֶל רָחֵל. And his saying <b>and he loved also Rachel</b> means that besides the addition spoken of before, He further added to him that he loved her more than Leah. And you will find that according to nature, constant habituation diminishes love, as Scripture says (Proverbs 25:17): ״פֶּן יִשְׂבָּעֲךָ וּשְׂנֵאֶךָ״, while distance breeds desire, and this is the sod of (Proverbs 9:17): ״מַיִם גְּנוּבִים יִמְתָּקוּ״. Therefore Scripture hints by saying <b>and he came also to Rachel</b> with added permanence — and even so, he also loved her, and the love did not diminish on this account, despite his being constant with her and not with Leah. And the measure of the word <b>from Leah</b> is: more than [he loved] Leah."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "Cited as Genesis Rabbah צח:ד in the source; the standard chapter–verse reference for this passage is Bereshit Rabbah 70:3."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:57.112923666Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}